ture/src/Cipher.crysl]","Oracle's JCA Reference Guide recommends fully specifying an algorithm, mode, and padding in a transformation rather than using an algorithm-only transformation, because algorithm-only use selects provider defaults. The guide notes that SunJCE and SunPKCS11 defaults for many symmetric ciphers include ECB and PKCS5Padding. [Official guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","For parameter-requiring ciphers, the JCA guide documents that encryption initialization without supplied parameters can cause a provider to generate them, while decryption initialization without required parameters causes `InvalidKeyException` or `InvalidAlgorithmParameterException`. The same parameters must be used for decryption. Parameters can be obtained using `Cipher.getParameters()` or, for an IV, `Cipher.getIV()`. [Official guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","For AEAD modes, the `Cipher` API requires all additional authenticated data (AAD) to be supplied with `updateAAD` before ciphertext is processed by `update` or `doFinal`. The JCA guide's AES-GCM example states that decryption uses the same AAD and GCM parameters, and that an IV must change when the same key is reused for another encryption. [Official API and guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/Cipher.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","The official `Cipher` and JCA documentation reviewed does not prescribe a remediation for an analyzer's inability to establish a CrySL key-origin predicate. Its documented API-level corrective guidance is to use a fully specified supported transformation, an appropriate key and operation mode, required parameters for decryption, AAD before AEAD ciphertext processing, and changed GCM IV parameters for further encryption with a reused key. [Official guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","`Cipher` does not define a general self-describing ciphertext, persisted-record, or wire-format container. The JCA guide documents transferring encoded `AlgorithmParameters`, reconstructing them, and providing them at decryption initialization. For AES-GCM it also requires the same AAD and GCM parameters at decryption. Consequently, the documentation supplies requirements for retaining/reconstructing applicable encryption metadata, but no automatic ciphertext-format conversion or generic migration mechanism when a transformation changes. [Official guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","For wrapped keys, the JCA guide documents that the recipient needs the wrapped key's algorithm name and its type (`SECRET_KEY`, `PRIVATE_KEY`, or `PUBLIC_KEY`) in order to unwrap it. [Official guide: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html]","`Cipher` has existed since Java 1.4; `Cipher.updateAAD` and `GCMParameterSpec` since Java 7. Oracle documented SunJCE AES/GCM/NoPadding and GCM AlgorithmParameters support as a JDK 8 security enhancement. In Java SE 26, AES/GCM/NoPadding is a required standard transformation; support for a transformation on an earlier target or by a particular provider must be determined from that target/provider's documentation. [Official API and release documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/Cipher.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/GCMParameterSpec.html ; https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/security/enhancements-8.html]","The primary `Cipher.crysl` source scopes its rule to the receiver/API type with the verbatim clause: `SPEC javax.crypto.Cipher`. Thus, the source declares a specification for `javax.crypto.Cipher`; it does not state a generic name-only matcher such as `*.init(...)`. [Primary rule: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Cipher.crysl]","The same source declares `java.security.Key key;` and models these key-taking initialization parameter lists verbatim: `i3: init(encmode, key);`, `i4: init(encmode, key, paramSpec);`, `i5: init(encmode, key, params);`, `i6: init(encmode, key, paramSpec, random);`, `i7: init(encmode, key, params, random);`, and `i8: init(encmode, key, random);`. It therefore constrains the parameter shapes of the listed `init` events, rather than stating that every method named `init` is an event. [Primary rule: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Cipher.crysl]","For the reported key-origin condition, the primary rule evaluates this verbatim condition: `generatedKey[key, alg(transformation)] || generatedPubkey[key] || generatedPrivkey[key];`. This is the rule-source condition concerning its declared `key` variable. [Primary rule: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Cipher.crysl]","The rule source also declares `getInstance(transformation)`, `getInstance(transformation, _)`, selected `updateAAD`, `update`, `doFinal`, and `wrap(Key)` event forms. Its source text alone does not define the detector's type resolution, overload resolution, dispatch, or behavior for unresolved calls. Therefore, from the source alone, it cannot be stated that a method merely sharing an API method name can match; the text scopes the specification to `javax.crypto.Cipher` and supplies no generic name-only matching clause. [Primary rule: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Cipher.crysl]","None of the supplied imports declares an identifier named `mCipher`. In particular, `import javax.crypto.Cipher;` imports the `javax.crypto.Cipher` type, not an application field, local variable, or parameter named `mCipher`.","The Android `Cipher` API declares `getInstance(String transformation)`. Its argument is a transformation name. Consequently, an expression passed directly as that argument, including `mCipher`, must be compatible with `String`; the supplied imports do not establish `mCipher`'s declaration or declared type.","The supplied finding metadata and imports do not provide `mCipher`'s declaration, initializer, assignments, branches, or referenced configuration. Therefore, no exact runtime transformation string value can be confirmed from the provided material.","The names `org.fedorahosted.freeotp.encryptor.EncryptedKey`, `encrypt`, `CipherSpec`, and the quoted detector message do not identify a published standard, protocol, file format, or external service that fixes a cipher transformation or key parameter."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The supplied branch URL is not commit-pinned, and the reviewed primary source alone does not verify that its content has SHA-256 `da73b53a10fc760da823ae81250e204a4d20a0566a38603e1938db9489d6ad92`.","No target Java release, JCA provider, selected transformation, initialization overload, key implementation, parameter values, or persisted/wire-data format was supplied. Official documentation therefore cannot establish which transformations are supported in the target environment or what compatibility properties apply to a particular format.","The CrySL source does not define the analysis engine's type resolution, dispatch, dataflow, or predicate-production semantics. It therefore does not resolve how a specific detector treats unresolved calls or why it did not establish an accepted predicate for a specific key value.","Official JCA documentation does not define CrySL's `generatedKey`, `generatedPubkey`, or `genera